/** @page common_pitfalls Common Pitfalls

    @tableofcontents

    This page is an index of %Qore behaviors that are correct and documented but that commonly
    surprise programmers coming from other languages.  Each entry states the rule briefly and links
    to the reference section that covers it in full; nothing here is unique to this page.

    The entries share a shape: the behavior is not an error, so the parser accepts the code, and the
    consequence appears somewhere else — usually at run time, and usually only on the input that
    triggers it.  That is what makes them worth collecting in one place.

    @section pitfalls_index Index by Symptom

    |!What you see|!Cause|!Read
    |<tt>RUNTIME-TYPE-ERROR: &lt;lvalue&gt; expects type 'int', but got no value instead</tt>|a declared variable was read before being assigned; a declaration does not initialize|@ref declaration_vs_initialization
    |<tt>PARSE-TYPE-ERROR: ... the container's element type was inferred from an earlier assignment</tt>|a \c hash<auto> or \c list<auto> was narrowed by an assignment|@ref auto_no_narrow
    |a \c map result fails to satisfy a \c list&lt;T&gt; lvalue, but only sometimes|the source was @ref nothing, so \c map returned @ref nothing rather than an empty list|@ref map
    |a hash key that is present tests as absent|the key's value is an empty hash or list, which is @ref False|@ref hash_key_presence_tests
    |a value compares equal to both a real value and "missing"|@ref NULL is not @ref NOTHING; <tt>exists NULL</tt> is @ref True|@ref null
    |a method override is silently ignored|another branch of the <tt>inherits</tt> clause is listed first|@ref multiple_inheritance_order
    |a subclass reads a parent member as empty, with no error|the member is <tt>private:internal</tt> and is invisible to subclasses|@ref pitfalls_private_internal
    |an interval drifts by an hour, or exceeds a remote limit only in some time zones|\c D and \c M are calendar components, not fixed durations|@ref date_time_arithmetic_calendar_components
    |<tt>PARSE-EXCEPTION: hard keyword 'enum' cannot be used after '.'</tt>|a dot member name collided with a @ref keywords "hard keyword"|@ref pitfalls_keywords
    |<tt>PARSE-EXCEPTION: 'C()' names class 'C', not a function</tt>|an object constructor was written without \c new|@ref pitfalls_new
    |a character loop truncates non-ASCII text|@ref <string>::size() "size()" counts bytes, not characters|@ref pitfalls_size_length

    @section pitfalls_types Types and Declarations

    @subsection pitfalls_declaration Declaring a variable does not initialize it
    <tt>int i;</tt> leaves \c i with no value, not \c 0.  Reads mostly appear to work because
    @ref nothing converts to \c 0, \c "" or @ref False as needed, so the failure arrives only where
    the declared type is actually required.  See @ref declaration_vs_initialization.

    @subsection pitfalls_narrowing Container types are narrowed by the first assignment
    <tt>hash&lt;auto&gt; h = {"x": 1};</tt> makes \c h a <tt>hash&lt;string, int&gt;</tt>, so <tt>h.y = "s";</tt>
    is a parse error.  A <i>heterogeneous</i> initial value does not narrow, which is why this only
    bites sometimes.  Declare the variable <tt>hash&lt;auto!&gt;</tt> or <tt>list&lt;auto!&gt;</tt> when it
    genuinely holds mixed types; see @ref auto_no_narrow.

    @subsection pitfalls_null NULL is not NOTHING
    @ref NULL is the SQL \c NULL value and is a value: <tt>exists NULL</tt> is @ref True and
    <tt>NULL === NOTHING</tt> is @ref False.  @ref NOTHING is the absence of a value.  Database
    columns and protocol payloads distinguish the two; see @ref null and @ref nothing.

    @section pitfalls_containers Containers

    @subsection pitfalls_presence Presence, emptiness, and absence are three different things
    An empty hash or list is @ref False, so a truth test cannot tell a missing key from a key whose
    value is empty.  Use @ref <hash>::hasKey() "hasKey()" when presence is what carries the
    meaning; see @ref hash_key_presence_tests.

    @subsection pitfalls_map_nothing map over NOTHING returns NOTHING
    A @ref nothing source yields @ref nothing, while an empty list yields an empty list.  Coerce with
    <tt>?? ()</tt> where the result must satisfy a typed list; see @ref map.

    @section pitfalls_operators Operators and Functions

    @anchor pitfalls_size_length
    @subsection pitfalls_size size() counts bytes; length() counts characters
    Bounding a character loop with @ref <string>::size() "size()" truncates multi-byte text and reads
    past the end of the string.  Use @ref <string>::length() "length()" for a character count; see
    @ref character_encoding.

    @subsection pitfalls_split split() does not split a string into characters
    <tt>split("", str)</tt> returns a one-element list holding the whole string.  Iterate characters
    with @ref <string>::length() "length()" and @ref <string>::substr() "substr()" instead.  Note
    also that the third parameter of @ref Qore::split() "split()" is \c with_separator, which
    <i>keeps</i> each separator attached to its token — it is not a "keep trailing empty" flag.

    @subsection pitfalls_int_division Integer division truncates toward zero
    <tt>int / int</tt> is integer division: <tt>7 / 2</tt> is \c 3 and <tt>-7 / 2</tt> is \c -3.
    Truncation is toward zero, not toward negative infinity, so flooring a negative quotient needs an
    explicit correction.

    @subsection pitfalls_string_repeat Multiplication is always numeric
    <tt>"0" * 64</tt> converts the string to a number and evaluates to the integer \c 0; it does not
    repeat the string.  Use @ref Qore::strmul() "strmul()" to repeat a string.

    @section pitfalls_dates Dates and Intervals

    @subsection pitfalls_calendar Days and months are calendar units
    Adding \c 92D preserves the wall-clock time and so can advance the clock by 2209 hours across a
    daylight savings time transition, while \c 2208h always advances it by exactly 2208 hours.  Any
    value that must be a fixed elapsed duration belongs in hours or seconds; see
    @ref date_time_arithmetic_calendar_components.

    @section pitfalls_classes Classes

    @subsection pitfalls_mi_order The first inherits branch wins
    When two inherited branches supply the same method, the one listed first in the
    <tt>inherits</tt> clause is used.  Reordering the clause changes which implementation runs; see
    @ref multiple_inheritance_order.

    @anchor pitfalls_private_internal
    @subsection pitfalls_private private is not private
    In %Qore, <tt><b>private</b></tt> restricts access to the class hierarchy — it is what other
    languages call <i>protected</i> — so a <tt><b>private</b></tt> method can be overridden by a
    subclass.  <tt><b>private:internal</b></tt> is the genuinely private form.  A subclass that
    references a <tt>private:internal</tt> parent member gets no parse error: the name simply does
    not resolve to that member, so a loop over it iterates zero times and a lookup always reports
    "not found".  See @ref private_methods.

    @section pitfalls_syntax Syntax and Keywords

    @anchor pitfalls_keywords
    @subsection pitfalls_reserved Identifiers that collide with keywords
    @ref keywords "Keywords" such as \c sub, \c elements, \c keys, \c context and \c final are
    accepted as hash member names after the @ref dot_operator ". operator", but \c enum is a hard
    keyword and is not.  This bites when mirroring an external field name — a JSON Schema \c enum
    field, or OpenID Connect's \c sub — into %Qore code.  Write the key as a quoted subscript
    (<tt>h{"enum"}</tt>) whenever it could collide. The parser reports the exact \c .enum span and
    supplies that quoted-subscript replacement to diagnostic tools.

    @subsection pitfalls_new Constructing an object in an expression requires new
    <tt>C().f()</tt> is parsed as a call to a function named \c C. When the name resolves to a class,
    the parser gives <tt>PARSE-EXCEPTION: 'C()' names class 'C', not a function</tt> and supplies a
    machine-applicable insertion of \c new. Write <tt>(new C()).f()</tt>, or assign the object to a
    variable first. Qualified class names such as <tt>Ns::C()</tt> receive the same feedback.

    @subsection pitfalls_const_type A read-only local variable needs an explicit type
    <tt>const X = "v";</tt> inside a method body is rejected with
    <tt>read-only local variable 'X' declared without type information</tt>.  Write
    <tt>const string X = "v";</tt> or <tt>const auto X = "v";</tt>; see
    @ref readonly_local_variables.
*/
